+# Phase 5 Plan 03: Citation marker grammar and parser Summary
+
+Pure TypeScript marker grammar (`[S3]`, `[S3, S5]`, `[S3][S5]`, `【S3】`, `［S3］`, `[S3，S5]`, `[S3、S5]`) in one shared module, plus an incremental parser that finds and validates the same markers whatever the token boundaries.
+
+## What was built
+
+- `src/lib/citations/marker-grammar.ts` (no imports): `matchMarkerAt` is a small state machine returning `match`, `partial` (valid prefix, more input could complete it) or `none`. `findCitationMarkers` runs it over the text. `stripCitationMarkers` removes every marker and the spaces or tabs before it. `neutralizeCitationMarkers` inserts U+200B between `S` and the digits of every marker, and returns the identical string when there is no marker.
+- `src/lib/rag/generation/citations/marker-parser.ts` (imports only the grammar): `createMarkerParser({aliases})` returns `{feed(textId, delta), end(textId), finish()}`. It keeps one tail buffer per text id (at most 17 characters, the opener plus 16) and scans `tail + delta`. `finish()` returns `{valid, invalidCitations, invalidAliases}`; valid aliases are listed once in first-appearance order, every invalid occurrence is counted, `invalidAliases` is the distinct list. `parseAnswerMarkers(text, aliases)` is the one-shot form.
